<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Trip to the Beach</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;"><b>Theme:</b> I Always Pack Lunch.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Demonstrate comprehension of simple written exchanges in letters, emails, or social media. reading2: Create lists from information read in texts. phonemicAwareness: Read regularly spelled one-syllable or two-syllable words represented by single letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can demonstrate comprehension by identifying steps in written exchanges about trip planning (e.g., 'Please, pack a hat.'). Can locate key information in written checklists for trips (e.g., 'Take a towel.'). Can recognize and decode regularly spelled, one-syllable, or two-syllable words related to beach vocabulary (e.g., /p/ /æk/ in 'pack,' /s/ /ændi/ in 'sandy').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different items for a beach trip (e.g., a swimsuit, a towel, sunscreen, a cooler, a beach ball, flip-flops, a sun hat). Teacher asks: "What do you see?" and "Do you take this to the beach?" Students identify the items. Teacher prompts: "What do you always pack for the beach?" (Pragmatic: Describing trips: 'I always pack lunch for the trip.')</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson, focusing on one-syllable or two-syllable words, especially 'pack' /pæk/ and 'beach' /biːtʃ/, emphasizing the /b/ and /p/ sounds. The teacher also models how to look at a simple written message and identify the topic (e.g., "Look at the title and pictures first to understand the main idea.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For 'pack': "Do I *pack* my lunch in my hand or in a bag?" (Expected answer: "In a bag.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For 'sunscreen': "Is sunscreen for the sun or for the rain?" (Expected answer: "For the sun.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Trip to the Beach".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated email or text message from a child named Leo to a friend, Mia, about a planned beach trip. The text includes simple sentences using present simple, imperatives, and 'going to'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Text:</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Subject: Our Beach Trip!</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Hi Mia,</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>I am going to the beach this Saturday! It's going to be sunny and fun. I always pack a big picnic lunch. Please, bring your swimsuit and a towel. Don't forget your sunscreen and a sun hat! We can build sandcastles and collect shells. Do you like to swim? See you there!</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Love, Leo</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Grammar: Present simple, Imperatives, Going to. Vocabulary: beach, sunny, fun, pack, picnic, swimsuit, towel, sunscreen, sun hat, build, collect, shells, swim. Pragmatic: Describing trips, Giving instructions politely. Phonemic Awareness: 'pack' /pæk/, 'beach' /biːtʃ/)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding simple written exchanges in social media or emails about trip planning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> Teacher asks: "Who is going to the beach?" (Leo and Mia) "When are they going?" (This Saturday) "What is the main idea of Leo's message?" (Planning a beach trip).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read sentences about the email and mark them true or false. E.g., "Leo is going to the mountains." (False). "Mia needs to bring a towel." (True). Teacher asks students to point to the part of the text that supports their answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students read a short list of items (e.g., swimsuit, sunscreen, picnic) and draw a line to connect them with who needs to bring them (Leo or Mia) or what they are for.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading, targeting comprehension and decoding skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying imperatives and target vocabulary related to beach items. Decoding regularly spelled one- or two-syllable words (e.g., 'sand,' 'swim,' 'beach,' 'pack,' 'sandy').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Reading Comprehension & Vocabulary):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with the email text, but with certain key vocabulary words (e.g., 'swimsuit,' 'towel,' 'sunscreen,' 'pack,' 'beach') removed. Students read the text and choose the correct word from a word bank to fill in the blanks. This reinforces reading comprehension and vocabulary recognition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice (Reading Imperatives & Phonemic Awareness):</b> The teacher writes a list of imperative phrases from the text on flashcards (e.g., "Please, bring your swimsuit.", "Don't forget your sun hat!"). Students read each phrase aloud, focusing on clear pronunciation of words like 'bring' /brɪŋ/ and 'forget' /fərˈɡɛt/, and then match it to a corresponding picture or action. The teacher highlights spelling and blending of simple one-syllable words like 'hat' and 'swim'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check (Reading for Specific Information):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a short "beach packing checklist" with 5-6 items. One student reads an item from the checklist (e.g., "Take a towel."). The other student points to a picture of the item. Then they switch roles. This activity encourages reading for key information and recognizing regularly spelled words from the vocabulary list. (Learning Outcome: Can locate key information in written checklists for trips).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Trip to the Beach."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative list creation from a written text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher provides each group with a new short written note (e.g., from a family member) about packing for a different beach trip. This note includes details about what each person *needs* to pack or what items are *already packed*. For example: "Mom says: 'Please pack your flip-flops. Dad is bringing the cooler. We need a big umbrella.' " (Grammar: Imperatives, Present Simple. Vocabulary: flip-flops, cooler, umbrella.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Each group reads their note carefully.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Together, they create a 'What WE Need to Pack' list based ONLY on the information they read in their note.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Students write down the items, practicing spelling of target words (e.g., 'beach,' 'sandals,' 'fun' – phonemic awareness).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. One student from each group reads their list aloud to the class, while other groups check if they included only items from their note.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This activity directly aligns with the specific objective "Create lists from information read in texts" and the learning outcome "Can locate key information in written checklists for trips".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check learning outcomes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group task, focusing on student's ability to locate and extract information from text, and a quick individual exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher gives each student a short, simple written message (e.g., from a friend planning a beach picnic: "Hello! I am going to the beach. I always pack lunch with sandwiches and juice. Please bring fruit and water.") Students read the message individually and create a short list of "Things to Bring" mentioned in the text. (Learning Outcome: Can create lists from information read in texts. Can demonstrate comprehension by identifying steps in written exchanges).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate identification and listing of items mentioned in the text (e.g., fruit, water).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of the written list.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct spelling of target vocabulary words (e.g., 'fruit,' 'water,' 'lunch').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "What did you learn to pack for the beach today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "Was it easy or difficult to find the information in the text?" (Circle one: Easy / Difficult)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and connection to the next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher prompts students with:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new beach words can you read now?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part of reading about the beach trip?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students effectively locate key information and create lists from the written texts? The reading level was appropriate for A1.2, and the illustrations helped comprehension."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The phonemic awareness activities with words like 'pack' and 'sandy' helped reinforce decoding skills. Some students still need more practice with blending two-syllable words."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I will continue to provide scaffolding for understanding imperative forms and distinguishing them from simple statements."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> "Great job reading about our beach trip! Next time, we will practice *speaking* about our beach plans and what we *always* pack for a fun day!" (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>